<style>
  /* NO TRANSITION ON THE BACKGROUND, and that is a fix rather than an
     omission. The bar's legibility depends on two things agreeing: the ground
     (a background, which a transition animates over 240ms) and the ink (a set
     of custom properties, which flip in one frame because custom properties
     do not transition). While they disagree the nav is paper-on-paper — and
     under rAF throttling, which is exactly when a scroll is being animated,
     that window stretches. Observed on the live site: a paper bar with
     near-invisible links. Two mechanisms with different clocks cannot be
     tuned into agreement; they can only be collapsed into one. So the ground
     switches in the same frame the ink does. */
  .site-header { position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: var(--z-sticky); }
  /* And the ink is instant too, for the same reason: a colour transition on
     the links is a 120ms nicety, and during it the ink is somewhere between
     the two ramps while the ground has already committed to one of them. One
     frame of paper-on-paper is not perceptible, but it is the same defect in
     miniature, and there is nothing to weigh against removing it. The hover
     transition stays — hover does not change the ground. */
  .site-header.scrolled .nav-links > li > a,
  .site-header.scrolled .nav-trigger,
  .site-header.scrolled .nav-account-link,
  .site-header.scrolled .lang-switch .ls,
  body:has(.hero-cover, .hero-editorial, .hv-hero) .site-header:not(.scrolled) .nav-links > li > a,
  body:has(.hero-cover, .hero-editorial, .hv-hero) .site-header:not(.scrolled) .nav-trigger,
  body:has(.hero-cover, .hero-editorial, .hv-hero) .site-header:not(.scrolled) .nav-account-link,
  body:has(.hero-cover, .hero-editorial, .hv-hero) .site-header:not(.scrolled) .lang-switch .ls { transition: none; border-radius: var(--r-pill); }
  /* --r-pill, not --r-sm. This selector is longer than the base `.nav-trigger`
     rule and therefore wins, which is how the header's dropdown trigger stayed
     at 8px while every button beside it went round — found by walking the
     cascade in a browser, not by reading. Anything in this bar that can be
     pressed now shares one shape. */
  /* The scrolled bar was a translucent dark pane with backdrop-filter: blur(10).
     Both are gone. The blur re-rendered its backdrop every frame while the page
     moved under it — the single most expensive thing in the sticky header — and
     a frosted pane is the glassmorphism default this system rejects. A solid
     paper bar with a hairline under it is cheaper, hard-edged, and legible over
     any content, including a photograph. */
  .site-header.scrolled { background: rgb(8 9 11 / 0.86); backdrop-filter: saturate(140%) blur(14px); box-shadow: 0 1px 0 var(--line); }
  /* The espresso ground's scrolled bar. This lives HERE, beside the rule it
     overrides, rather than in global.css — Astro adds a data-astro-cid
     attribute to every selector in this block, so a plain
     `.ground-espresso .site-header.scrolled` in global.css has the same
     specificity as the scoped rule above and loses on order. Measured: a
     white slab with paper ink on it, invisible for the whole page rather
     than for one frame. Same class of defect as section 16's, in a new
     place, caught the same way — by reading the rendered colour. */
  :global(body.ground-espresso) .site-header.scrolled { background: rgb(8 9 11 / 0.86); box-shadow: 0 1px 0 var(--line); }

  /* ---- the header over a dark hero -------------------------------------
     MEASURED BUG, not a refinement: on every page that opens with a
     photographic hero, the un-scrolled header kept the PAPER side of the ink
     ramp and painted it onto the photograph. Sampled against the rendered
     pixels behind each link, the nav ran at 1.29–1.74:1. It is the one
     element on the site a contrast checker cannot catch by reading tokens,
     because by token it is correct — --ink-2 on --bg is 5.75:1, and --bg is
     simply not what is behind it.

     The header carries no ground of its own until it scrolls, so the fix is
     to give it the ground's ramp for as long as it is standing on one. The
     scrim under it is what supplies the plate; these tokens supply the ink.

     :has() rather than a JS class, because a nav that is only legible once a
     script has run is a nav that is illegible for the first paint and for
     anyone with JS off. The @supports fallback below trades the transparent
     look for a solid bar — less handsome, still readable, which is the right
     way round for a fallback.

     THE SELECTOR IS A LIST OF HERO CLASS NAMES, which is a maintenance edge
     worth naming: section 17's rebuilt hero is .hv-hero, and until it was
     added here the account link measured 2.73:1 against the brightest pixel
     of the photograph — the fix was in place and simply did not know about
     the new hero. Any future full-bleed hero goes in this list on the same
     commit that creates it. */
  body:has(.hero-cover, .hero-editorial, .hv-hero) .site-header:not(.scrolled) {
    /* ONE ink tier, not three. Measured against the actual rendered pixels
       behind each link (text hidden, screenshot sampled, worst pixel taken),
       the second tier ran 1.62–4.09:1 and the third 2.78:1. A 76px bar over a
       photograph the studio does not control the exposure of has room for one
       legible value, and hierarchy in the nav comes from weight and position
       instead. This is the one place on the site where a ramp is deliberately
       flattened, and it is flattened because the ground is unknowable. */
    --ink:        var(--paper-on-dark);
    --ink-2:      var(--paper-on-dark);
    --ink-3:      var(--paper-on-dark);
    --ink-faint:  var(--paper-soft-on-dark);
    /* The language switcher is a filled chip: without these it kept a paper
       fill while its label flipped to paper — white on white, 1.05:1, on the
       control that tells you which language you are reading. */
    /* An INK alpha, not a paper one. A paper-alpha shell lightens whatever
       photograph is behind it, which is the wrong direction: it lifted the
       ground under the inactive chip to 3.77:1 against its own paper label.
       Darkening the shell instead helps both chips at once. */
    --surface:    rgb(var(--scrim) / 0.44);
    --surface-2:  rgb(var(--scrim) / 0.30);
    --line:        rgb(255 255 255 / 0.16);
    --line-strong: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.30);
    --line-ui:     var(--paper-muted-on-dark);
    /* THE LOGOTYPE FLATTENS TO PAPER HERE TOO, and this is the one scope where
       it does not follow the accent. :root moved --brand to #C6F100 in August
       2026 — green reads at 15.16:1 on the page ground and the mark is green
       everywhere else, including the footer and the scrolled bar.

       Over a hero photograph it is not. Measured exactly the way the ink tiers
       above were (wordmark hidden, the box it occupies screenshotted, the
       BRIGHTEST pixel taken as the ground):

         /                5.55:1   ✓
         /how-it-works    3.70:1   ✗
         /video           3.19:1   ✗
         /about           2.94:1   ✗

       Three of the four fail, and they fail because the green is a mid-tone:
       it has the room white has against a dark photograph and none of white's
       room against a bright one. So the bar over a hero keeps the paper mark
       and swaps to green at the same moment the bar itself goes solid — one
       transition, not two, which is the only reason a logotype changing colour
       is acceptable rather than a bug.

       Re-take these numbers when a hero photograph is replaced. Nothing will
       report it: a contrast checker reads text and this is an <svg>. */
    --brand:       var(--paper-on-dark);
    --focus-ring:  var(--paper-on-dark);
    --ghost:       rgb(255 255 255 / 0.12);
    --btn-ghost-fill:       rgb(255 255 255 / 0.12);
    --btn-ghost-fill-hover: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.20);
    --accent-soft: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.14);
    --accent-line: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.34);
    /* SOLID paper, not another alpha. Two stacked paper alphas (the switcher
       shell at 0.14 and the chip at 0.16) composited to a mid grey and put
       paper text on it at 2.03–2.20:1. A selected chip on a dark ground is
       the inverse of the ground, not a slightly-lighter version of it. */
    --chip-fill:   var(--paper-on-dark);
    --chip-ink:    var(--ink-900);
  }
  @supports not selector(:has(*)) {
    .site-header { background: var(--paper); box-shadow: 0 1px 0 var(--line-strong); }
  }
  .nav { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1.5rem; height: 76px; }
  .brand { font-family: var(--font-body); font-weight: 800; font-size: 1.26rem; color: var(--ink); display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: .5rem; }
  /* The logotype, flat. It was chrome-filled — the same twelve-stop ramp as the
     hero plane, remapped per ground — and it went to one ink, on the client's
     call: at logo size the metal read as cheap rather than as expensive, which
     is the opposite of what the material was there to say. Nothing about the
     size reasoning changed with it. 13px was smaller than the cap height of the
     nav labels beside it; 17px in a 76px bar is still restrained and now sits
     level with them. The footer gets 24px because that is where the mark has
     room to be looked at.

     Task #272, 2026-07-30: fill: var(--brand), not var(--ink) any more — the
     harbor style guide's teal, one flat colour in the same slot the flat ink
     used to sit in. This is not the chrome reversal the paragraph above warns
     about; it is still one solid fill, not two and not a gradient, so the
     "read as cheap" failure that killed chrome has nothing to attach to here.
     --brand existed already, aliased to --ink-900, reserved and unread until
     now — see global.css for the value, the contrast numbers, and why
     --accent (the actual "no marketing accent" rule) is untouched. */
  .brand-word { height: 17px; width: auto; display: block; fill: var(--brand); }
  .site-footer .brand-word { height: 24px; }
  /* The monogram is the same material as the word, so it is the same mechanism:
     a fill-less symbol taking --brand from whatever ground it lands on. It
     replaces logo-mark.webp, which was a cyan-to-periwinkle raster — the last
     saturated colour left on the site after the monochrome pass, and measured
     at 1.39:1 against the mobile drawer's paper ground, i.e. a logo you cannot
     see. A raster also cannot flip per ground, so that could not be fixed by
     recolouring the file. --brand is a single fixed value rather than a
     ground-flipping one like --ink, which is why it was checked against BOTH
     grounds directly (5.41:1 on --paper, 3.29:1 on --ink-900 — every dark
     ground on this site, .on-ink's whole point, shares that one background)
     instead of being assumed safe the way --ink's flip guarantees. */
  .brand-mark { width: auto; display: block; fill: var(--brand); }
  /* 30px in a 76px bar. Sized against the CAP HEIGHT of the nav links beside
     it rather than against the bar: a mark set to the bar's own proportions
     towers over 15px type. 30px puts the glyph's optical weight level with the
     link row, which is what makes a chrome row read as one line rather than as
     a logo with some text next to it. Drops with the bar on a phone. */
  .brand-glyph { height: 30px; }
  @media (max-width: 700px) { .brand-glyph { height: 26px; } }
  /* SVG fill is not force-adjusted by the browser the way `color` is, so
     forced-colours still has to be told the ink by name or the mark keeps a
     colour the user asked to be rid of — CanvasText, not CanvasText via
     --brand, because a forced-colours user has already opted out of the
     site's palette entirely and --brand is exactly that. Print stays #000 for
     the same reason it always did: a printer lays down black reliably, and a
     printed page has no dark ground to resolve a footer mark against. */
  @media (forced-colors: active) { .brand-word, .brand-mark { fill: CanvasText; } }
  @media print { .brand-word, .brand-mark { fill: #000; } }
  .nav-links { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .1rem; list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  /* Pill, like everything else pressable in this bar. A 14px trigger standing
     next to a fully round Start button is the specific "blokkerig" Lucas
     pointed at — the header is where the two shapes sit closest together. */
  .nav-links > li > a, .nav-trigger { border-radius: var(--r-pill); display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: .35rem; padding: .55rem .8rem; font-size: .92rem; font-weight: 500; color: var(--ink-2); background: none; border: none; cursor: pointer; transition: color var(--dur-1) var(--ease); font-family: var(--font-body); }
  .nav-links > li > a:hover, .nav-trigger:hover { color: var(--ink); }
  .nav-links > li > a[aria-current="page"] { color: var(--ink); }
  .nav-actions { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .6rem; }
  /* Returning-customer entry point into /account (task #257/#259) — a plain
     text link so it never competes with nav_start's button weight. Hidden at
     the mobile breakpoint alongside .btn/.lang-switch below; the drawer
     carries its own copy (.mobile-account-link). */
  .nav-account-link { font-size: .88rem; font-weight: 500; color: var(--ink-3); padding: .55rem .4rem; transition: color var(--dur-1) var(--ease); }
  .nav-account-link:hover, .nav-account-link[aria-current="page"] { color: var(--ink); }

  /* ---- the signed-in chrome ------------------------------------------------
     Both states are in the markup and one of them is `hidden` (see the nav).
     The UA rule for [hidden] sits in the user-agent origin, where any
     `display` declared here beats it — so every rule below has to exclude the
     hidden state itself or the two states would draw on top of each other.
     That is what :not([hidden]) is doing, and it is load-bearing rather than
     defensive. */
  .nav-account-in { display: none; align-items: center; gap: .1rem; }
  .nav-account-in:not([hidden]) { display: inline-flex; }
  /* The brand's own name, so it can be long. Truncated rather than allowed to
     push the CTA off the bar — the name is confirmation of who you are, not
     something anyone reads word by word here; the dashboard prints it in full. */
  .nav-account-name { max-width: 15ch; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; white-space: nowrap; color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .nav-signout { display: inline-flex; margin: 0; }
  /* A quiet control beside a quiet link. Sign out must be reachable — Lucas's
     ask was that the site knows you are signed in, and a state you cannot
     leave from the chrome is not really chrome that knows. It takes --r-sm,
     the smallest of the three tokens, because it is the smallest thing here;
     nothing on this site is allowed a square corner. */
  .nav-signout-btn {
    font: inherit; font-size: .78rem; font-weight: 500; letter-spacing: .02em;
    color: var(--ink-3); background: none; border: 0; cursor: pointer;
    padding: .5rem .45rem; border-radius: var(--r-sm);
    text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    transition: color var(--dur-1) var(--ease);
  }
  .nav-signout-btn:hover { color: var(--ink); }
  .mobile-account-in { display: none; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; gap: .2rem; }
  .mobile-account-in:not([hidden]) { display: flex; }
  .mobile-account-in .mobile-account-link { color: var(--ink); }
  .mobile-signout { margin: 0; }
  .mobile-signout .nav-signout-btn { font-size: .9rem; }
  .has-menu { position: relative; }
  .nav-menu { position: absolute; top: calc(100% + 10px); left: 0; min-width: 470px; background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: .6rem; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: .25rem; opacity: 0; visibility: hidden; transform: translateY(8px); transition: opacity var(--dur-2) var(--ease), transform var(--dur-2) var(--ease), visibility var(--dur-2) var(--ease); box-shadow: 6px 6px 0 0 rgb(var(--scrim) / 0.16); border-radius: var(--r-lg); }
  .has-menu:hover .nav-menu, .has-menu:focus-within .nav-menu, .has-menu.menu-open .nav-menu { opacity: 1; visibility: visible; transform: translateY(0); }
  .nav-menu a { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .15rem; padding: .7rem .85rem; }
  .nav-menu a:hover, .nav-menu a[aria-current="page"] { background: var(--surface-2); }
  .nav-menu .mi-title { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; font-size: .92rem; }
  .nav-menu .mi-desc { color: var(--ink-3); font-size: .8rem; line-height: 1.4; }
  .menu-toggle { display: none; width: 44px; height: 44px; align-items: center; justify-content: center; border: 1px solid var(--line-strong); background: none; cursor: pointer; }
  .menu-toggle span, .menu-toggle span::before, .menu-toggle span::after { content: ''; display: block; width: 18px; height: 2px; background: var(--ink); }
  .menu-toggle span::before { transform: translateY(-6px); } .menu-toggle span::after { transform: translateY(4px); }
  .btn-block { width: 100%; }

  /* Language switcher (EN · NL · DE). Desktop version lives in .nav-actions;
     a second copy sits inside the mobile drawer (.mobile-lang). */
  .lang-switch { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 2px; background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line-strong); padding: 3px; border-radius: var(--r-md); }
  /* 36x27 measured on all 84 pages — under the 40px minimum in both axes, on
     a control people hit while holding a phone. The visible chip keeps its
